 ],
 "literal": "With the army of kings losing their front and being crushed / holding a raised sword, strong-armed, becoming a single man / preventing them from transgressing and coming past him, to the great ocean / he is like a shore indeed, always / not only to those who went singing, but also / of the small village unable to sustain giving / he is of generous nature, mindful of ancient lineage!",
 "translation": "When the armies of kings are crushed and their battlefronts break, he stands alone, a single man with a raised sword in his strong hand. Preventing the enemy from passing beyond him, he is like a shore that restrains the great ocean. Not only to those who come singing his praise, but also to his small village that cannot afford to give, he is a man of generous nature, mindful of his ancient lineage!",
 "modern": "மூவேந்தர்களின் படைகள் போர்க்களத்தில் சிதைந்து அழியும்போது, கையில் ஏந்திய வாளுடன் வலிமை மிக்க ஒருவனாக அவன் தனித்து நிற்கிறான். பகைவர்கள் தன்னைத் தாண்டிச் செல்லாதவாறு தடுப்பதில், அவன் பெரிய கடலைத் தடுக்கும் கரையைப் போன்றவன் ஆவான். தன்னைப் பாடி வரும் இரவலர்களுக்கு மட்டுமன்றி, பிறருக்குக் கொடுக்கும் வளம் இல்லாத தன் சிறிய ஊருக்கும், தன் குலத்தின் பழம்பெருமையை நினைத்து வாரி வழங்கும் வள்ளல் தன்மை கொண்டவன் அவன்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Vaahai thinai and Mootin Mullai thurai, which praises the ancient martial lineage and bravery of a warrior. The poet, Madurai Kanakkayanar, describes a heroic warrior who stands alone on the battlefield with a raised sword, successfully blocking the advancing enemy forces. He is compared to a shore (aazhi) that restrains the vast, powerful ocean, symbolizing his immense strength and protective role. Beyond his martial valor, the warrior is also celebrated for his generosity; despite living in a small, resource-poor village (seerur), he continues to give abundantly to bards and his own community, honoring his family's ancient tradition of charity. Key words like 'aazhi' (shore/boundary), 'seerur' (small village), and 'vanmaiyone' (generous one) highlight the dual virtues of heroism and philanthropy.",
